WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.043
Find out how tightly you can crop a bird photo and still make the print you want.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:07.047
Cropping removes pixels around the subject.

00:00:07.047 --> 00:00:10.772
It does not, by itself, make the pixels you keep less sharp.

00:00:10.772 --> 00:00:17.648
A small bird that is a little soft in the original will still be a little
soft in the crop; it will simply be easier to see that softness when the

00:00:17.648 --> 00:00:18.978
bird fills more of the screen.

00:00:18.978 --> 00:00:21.986
The important habit is to crop from the full-size original.

00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:25.844
Suppose your original is six thousand pixels wide and four thousand high.

00:00:25.844 --> 00:00:33.724
If you first export a copy that is only two thousand and forty-eight pixels
wide, then crop that copy, you have already thrown away most of the

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:36.059
available pixels before you start framing the bird.

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:41.665
Enlarging the finished crop changes its output dimensions but does not
recover the original captured detail.

00:00:41.665 --> 00:00:47.600
Tools that synthesize new detail are a different kind of edit and may not
suit documentary or competition uses.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.455
Check the rules for the destination before relying on them.

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:54.594
Also avoid repeatedly opening and saving the same JPEG at a lossy setting.

00:00:54.594 --> 00:01:00.527
Keep the camera original, do your editing from that source, and make a
delivery copy for the place you are sharing it.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:04.159
A full-resolution crop means the retained area is not downscaled.

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:08.836
It does not mean a small crop still contains the original photograph's full
pixel count.

00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.959
Print size is a useful calculation rather than a mystery.

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:16.313
Divide the pixel dimensions by the intended pixels per inch.

00:01:16.313 --> 00:01:23.208
A crop that is three thousand pixels wide and two thousand high makes a ten
by six point seven inch print at three hundred pixels per inch.

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:25.184
The table gives several examples.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:29.236
These are dimensions, not a guarantee that every file will look equally
sharp.

00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:34.080
Focus, motion, noise, processing, paper and viewing distance still matter.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.661
A slightly softer photograph can work well as a modest print, while a large
file can disappoint if the bird was out of focus.

00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:43.064
There is an easy trap in the phrase half a crop.

00:01:43.064 --> 00:01:46.523
Halving the width and halving the height retains one quarter of the pixels.

00:01:46.523 --> 00:01:53.138
A six thousand by four thousand photograph becomes three thousand by two
thousand: six megapixels rather than twenty-four.

00:01:53.138 --> 00:01:55.693
That is still a useful image for many purposes.

00:01:55.693 --> 00:02:00.142
Try this calculation with a two thousand four hundred by three thousand
pixel crop.

00:02:00.142 --> 00:02:03.889
At three hundred pixels per inch, what print dimensions do you get?

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.478
Divide each side separately.

00:02:05.478 --> 00:02:07.336
The answer is eight by ten inches.

00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:13.186
If the print lab needs a different shape, allow for that additional crop
before deciding whether the file is large enough.

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:16.066
Let us use a simple example.

00:02:16.066 --> 00:02:20.127
Imagine a twenty-four megapixel original that is six thousand pixels wide.

00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:22.685
The bird is only six hundred pixels across.

00:02:22.685 --> 00:02:27.257
If you crop so the bird fills half the width, the crop is twelve hundred
pixels wide.

00:02:27.257 --> 00:02:30.889
If it fills one fifth, the crop is three thousand pixels wide.

00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:35.862
Suppose a destination requires at least three thousand pixels on the long
edge without enlargement.

00:02:35.862 --> 00:02:41.543
The tight twelve hundred pixel crop cannot meet that requirement just
because the original camera file was large.

00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:48.210
The wider three thousand pixel crop may meet the dimension requirement, but
you still need to decide whether its composition works.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.987
Take ten seconds to consider your own photograph.

00:02:50.987 --> 00:02:56.483
What is the smallest crop that keeps the composition you want and still has
enough pixels for its destination?

00:02:56.483 --> 00:03:02.506
If those goals conflict, consider a smaller print, a different image or a
wider environmental composition.

00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:12.506
Pause and consider your own photograph.

00:03:12.506 --> 00:03:15.621
The point is to make the destination part of the decision early.

00:03:15.621 --> 00:03:21.820
Pixel dimensions are a constraint you can check, not something to discover
after spending an hour on the finished export.

00:03:22.832 --> 00:03:29.021
Crop from the original, divide both pixel dimensions by the print lab's target, and leave room for its paper shape.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:36.734
For the slides, transcript and the full bird photography workshop, go to get BirdFrame dot com slash club workshop.
